Title: Can an object with uninitialized fields be safely added in std::vector?.
Body: <p>In the following program, default constructor of struct <code>A</code> does not initialize its field <code>v</code>. Then in a constant expression, <code>std::vector&lt;A&gt;</code> is emplaced with <code>A()</code> object:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;vector&gt;

struct A {
  constexpr A() noexcept {}
  int v;
};

constexpr bool f() {
    std::vector&lt;A&gt; as;
    as.reserve(1);
    as.emplace_back();
    return true;
}
static_assert( f() );
</code></pre>
<p>MSVC compiler complains about the reading of uninitialized variable:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;source&gt;(14): error C2131: expression did not evaluate to a constant
&lt;source&gt;(11): note: failure was caused by a read of an uninitialized symbol
&lt;source&gt;(11): note: see usage of 'A::v'
</code></pre>
<p>But both GCC and Clang are fine with the program. Online demo: <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/addx11aTT" rel="noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/addx11aTT</a></p>
<p>Which compiler is correct here?</p>

