Title: Using Context inside a Class Instance in React.js.
Body: <p>I'm trying to use <a href="https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns/template-method" rel="nofollow noreferrer">template design pattern</a>, to refactor a complex business logic in my React application. I have 3-4 flows where-in some steps are common, but have slightly different implementations or addition and intermediate steps.</p>
<p>For this I'm creating an abstract base class in TypeScript with concrete implementation of few steps. The Classes that will extend this abstract class needs to provide concrete implementation of the abstract methods defined inside base class.</p>
<p>Now, the problem/query I'm having is regarding using Context API and Hooks(like useHistory) inside these Plain TypeScript classes (that are not extending <code>React.Component</code>).</p>
<p>The code looks something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { MyContext } from './common/utils/MyContext';
import { useHistory } from 'react-router-dom';
import * as React from 'react';

const MyComponent = () =&gt; {
  const history = useHistory();
  const context = React.useContext(MyContext);

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (foo &amp;&amp; bar) {
      const flowInitializer1 = new flowInitializer1(history, context, foo, bar);
      flowInitializer1.initiate();
    } else if (foo &amp;&amp; bazz) {
      const flowInitializer2 = new flowInitializer2(history, context, foo, bazz);
      flowInitializer2.initiate();
    } else {
      const flowInitializer3 = new flowInitializer3(history, context);
      flowInitializer3.initiate();
      flowInitializer3.cleanup();
    }
  }, []);

  return (
    // some JSX
  );
}

abstract class BaseFlowInitializer {
    constructor(
      context: IContextType,
      history:  RouteComponentProps['history']
    ) {}

    step1() {
      this.context.foo();
      // some code 
    }

    step2() {
      // some code
      history.push('/fizzbuz');
    }

    abstract step3();

    abstract initiate();
}

export class flowInitializer1 extends BaseFlowInitializer {
  step3() {
    // concrete implementation
  }

  initiate() {
    // concrete implementation
  }
}

export class flowInitializer2 extends BaseFlowInitializer {
  step3() {
    // concrete implementation
  }

  step4() {

  }

  initiate() {
    // concrete implementation
  }
}

export class flowInitializer3 extends BaseFlowInitializer {
  step3() {
    // concrete implementation
  }

  cleanup() {
    // some additional step
  }

  initiate() {
    // concrete implementation
  }
}

</code></pre>
<p>My Questions now are:</p>
<ol>
<li>Is the above implementation recommended as per React standards?</li>
<li>Can the above code cause performance issues, by breaking the React Fibre?</li>
<li>Is there any better way to implement template design pattern in React.js?</li>
</ol>

Title: Unable to autowire Spring Boot components while using factory pattern.
Body: <p>I'm not able to inject Environment dependencies in the classes that extend the base interface in a factory pattern. My architect advised me to pass the dependency through method parameters, but now the requirement has changed to add more dependencies. I don't feel comfortable in adding more parameters to the same function anymore. The parameter change in one function has to be updated in base interface along with all the classes implementing it. Please suggest a better way of solving this issue.</p>
<p>I tried to add more parameters in the function to accommodate new dependencies, but this doesn't look like a scalable and clean approach.</p>
<p>Taking the example of this NotifierFactory:</p>
<pre><code>
public interface Notification {
    void notifyUser();
}

public class SMSNotification implements Notification {

    @Override
    public void notifyUser()
    {
        // TODO Auto-generated method stub
        System.out.println(&quot;Sending an SMS notification&quot;);
    }
}

public class EmailNotification implements Notification {

    @Override
    public void notifyUser()
    {
        // TODO Auto-generated method stub
        System.out.println(&quot;Sending an e-mail notification&quot;);
    }
}

public class PushNotification implements Notification {

    @Override
    public void notifyUser()
    {
        // TODO Auto-generated method stub
        System.out.println(&quot;Sending a push notification&quot;);
    }
}

public class NotificationFactory {
    public Notification createNotification(String channel)
    {
        if (channel == null || channel.isEmpty())
            return null;
        switch (channel) {
        case &quot;SMS&quot;:
            return new SMSNotification();
        case &quot;EMAIL&quot;:
            return new EmailNotification();
        case &quot;PUSH&quot;:
            return new PushNotification();
        default:
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(&quot;Unknown channel &quot;+channel);
        }
    }
}

public class NotificationService {
@Autowired
Environment env;
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        NotificationFactory notificationFactory = new NotificationFactory();
        Notification notification = notificationFactory.createNotification(&quot;SMS&quot;);
        notification.notifyUser();
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p>Now, say I need to use the Environment bean that is autowired in NotificationService inside notifyUser() method of EmailNotification class. If I try to do simple autowiring, the value of env is resolved to null. So the workaround I used was to pass env in notifiyUser() function like, notifyUser(env). The drawback of this approach is that now I need to change the definition of notifyUser() method in all classes implementing Notfication class, even though the env dependency is not used there. And in future, if I need to add more such dependencies, every time the function signature will change.</p>

Title: WebGPU Text Rendering.
Body: <p>How can I render text using pure WebGPU without third party libraries if possible?</p>
<p>I am still in the process of learning WebGPU and don't know much, but I found this solution, but it uses third-party libraries</p>
<pre><code>/* Canvas initialization */

const canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;) as
HTMLCanvasElement;

/* WebGPU initialization */

const adapter: GPUAdapter = await navigator.gpu.
requestAdapter() as GPUAdapter;
const device: GPUDevice = await adapter.requestDevice() as
  GPUDevice;
}

/* Font initialization */

const fontParser = new FontParser(device, &quot;fonts/RobotoRegular.ttf&quot;);

/* Text block to render */

const textBlock = new TextBlock(
    device, &quot;Hello World&quot;, fontParser,
    options = {
    color = [0.6, 0.5. 0.6, 1.0],
    spacing = 2,
    width = 2,
    size = 4,
    isWinding = false
});

/* 1) Initialization */

const renderer = new Renderer(device, canvas, textBlock,
projectionMatrix, viewMatrix, color=[0.2, 0.0, 0.0,1.0]);

/* 2) Preparing data */

const perFrameData = this.renderer.prepare();

/* 3) Rendering */

this.renderer.render(perFrameData);

</code></pre>
<p>I got it from here <a href="https://stackoverflow.com">https://is.muni.cz/th/nl4qv/Bachelor_s_Thesis.pdf</a></p>

