    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The reasons why I thought so, if I'm not wrong:</p>
<ol>
<li>Elements of automatic allocated array are all allocated in stack, so after function return I lost control of it.</li>
<li>Array variable is address of base element of this array in stack, so assignment of pointer passed as argument will point to that lost position in stack.</li>
</ol>
<p>But result is that I still have access to some memory, where array was allocated and I'm still able to change data of this array.</p>
