Title: How to deal with functions and arrays which might differ between embedded system instances (per client) without resorting to pre-processor abuse?.
Body: <p><strong>Description</strong>
I am the sole developer/designer/maintainer of an embedded system product for a controls company. I am a junior and there is no other engineer (of any discipline) in the company. The language is C (C99 standard) and the build system is CMake. The embedded system is a bare-metal STM32 MCU, with 8k RAM and 64k FLASH.
So far, the application has had one &quot;instance&quot;, a super-set of all the features.</p>
<p>For example, an <em>alarm-module</em>, fed by an enum of all possible alarm topics, receives updates on all relevant data-points, handles all possible alarm topic triggers, keeps track of how long each alarm topic has been active for, and every alarm topic has its own audible buzzer pattern.</p>
<p>Now however, we are starting to develop instances of this embedded system for different clients, each with their own sub-set of features. Within the scope of the alarm module, this could mean they say &quot;we don't care about ALARM2, but we do care about ALARM1 and ALARM3&quot;.</p>
<p>In an ideal case, such as a non-embedded system with much more RAM and non-volatile memory, the super-set modules would stay as is, and we simply have data in memory, functions, and paths that we never ever touch, or perhaps at run-time the system will read a configuration and allocate the appropriate memory. However, this is a constrained embedded system, and using malloc, having unnecessary RAM allocation, and dead code paths is not acceptable.</p>
<p>My goal is to introduce modularization such that there are core application modules (<em>alarm-module</em>) which link to a library (one per customer) of modules. This would mean we don't have to modify the application modules, but simply link it to the appropriate library for the appropriate customer.</p>
<p><strong>Concrete Example of the &quot;Super-Set&quot; Alarm Module</strong></p>
<pre><code>#include &quot;Counter.h&quot; //Count time
#include &quot;Buzzer.h&quot; //Produce audible alarm patterns

typedef enum  
{
ALARM_TOPIC_NONE = 0U,
ALARM_TOPIC_TEMPERATURE_RANGE,
ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE1_FAULT,
ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE2_FAULT,
ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE3_FAULT,
ALARM_TOPIC_EXTERNAL,
ALARM_TOPIC_EXT_SERIOUS,
ALARM_TOPIC_MAX, //Used to set array-lengths and loop conditions
} Alarm_Topics_E;

//Holds the current &quot;signal state&quot; of an alarm topic.
//Separate from &quot;raised state&quot; of an alarm topic
static bool alarm_conditions[ALARM_TOPIC_MAX] = {
[ALARM_TOPIC_NONE]              = true,
[ALARM_TOPIC_TEMPERATURE_RANGE] = false,
[ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE1_FAULT]      = false,
[ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE2_FAULT]      = false,
[ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE3_FAULT]      = false,
[ALARM_TOPIC_EXTERNAL]          = false,
[ALARM_TOPIC_EXT_SERIOUS]       = false,
};
static bool raised_alarms[ALARM_TOPIC_MAX] = {0};

//Holds all counter structs for each alarm topic
static Counter_T alarm_counters[ALARM_TOPIC_MAX] = {0};

static const Buzzer_Pattern_E alarm_pattern_table[ALARM_TOPIC_MAX] = {
[ALARM_TOPIC_NONE]              = BUZZER_PATTERN_QUIET,
[ALARM_TOPIC_TEMPERATURE_RANGE] = BUZZER_PATTERN1,
[ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE1_FAULT]  = BUZZER_PATTERN2,
[ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE2_FAULT]      = BUZZER_PATTERN3,
[ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE3_FAULT]      = BUZZER_PATTERN4,
[ALARM_TOPIC_EXTERNAL]          = BUZZER_PATTERN3,
[ALARM_TOPIC_EXT_SERIOUS]       = BUZZER_PATTERN5,
};

void alarm_event_handler(const Args_Generic_T *generic_args) {
//Extract information from arguments, such as alarm_topic
    switch(alarm_topic)
    {
        case ALARM_TOPIC_NONE:         {/*Do something1*/}
        case ALARM_TOPIC_TEMPERATURE_RANGE: {/*Do something2*/}
        case ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE1_FAULT: {/*Do something3*/}
        case ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE2_FAULT: {/*Do something4*/}
        case ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE3_FAULT: {/*Do something5*/}
        case ALARM_TOPIC_EXTERNAL:     {/*Do something6*/}
        case ALARM_TOPIC_EXT_SERIOUS:  {/*Do something7*/}
    }
}

/* Below are other methods which handle updating alarm_condition, raised_alarm, and alarm_counters.
These methods are agnostic to specifics about the Alarm_Topics enum. 
For example, the method which handles alarm counters might look like such */
static void alarm_handle_condition(Alarm_Topics_E topic, bool alarm_condition) {
    /* business logic */
    if (topic &lt; ALARM_TOPIC_MAX) {
        update_counter(&amp;alarm_counters[topic]);
    }
    /* what do ya know more business logic */
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now, since the list of alarm topics might change per customer, I put the Alarm_Topics_E enum into its own module, which can be maintained per customer. Let's call this module <em>customer-config</em>.</p>
<p>However, the super-set <em>alarm-module</em> has arrays which use specific members of the enum as array designators
<code>[ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE3_FAULT] = BUZZER_PATTERN2</code>
and methods which directly reference the enum members
<code>case ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE3_FAULT: {/*Do something5*/}</code>.</p>
<p>If the customer decided they did not need a third probe, how close can I get to simply removing
<code>ALARM_TOPIC_PROBE3_FAULT</code> from the <code>Alarm_Topics_E</code> enum in the <em>customer-config</em> module, and get the <em>alarm-module</em> to properly acknowledge that change?</p>
<p>If I simply removed the enum member, the code will obviously not compile.</p>
<p>If I used pre-processor definitions/compilation flags in my <em>customer-config</em> such as
<code>#define USE_PROBE3</code>
then the <em>alarm-module</em> will be full of</p>
<pre><code>#ifdef USE_PROBE3
/*activity related to probe3*/
#endif
</code></pre>
<p>If I moved the methods which are affected by this change into the <em>customer-config</em> module, and maintained them individually, I would also have to move multitudes of static-members of the <em>alarm-module</em> into the <em>customer-config</em> module, which don't feel like they belong there. Also, I would have to link the <em>customer-config</em> module against &quot;Buzzer.h&quot; which also doesn't feel right.</p>
<p>I am okay with potentially parsing a configuration structure on bootup, but I would still have to dynamically allocate array memory (HUGE NO-NO) and I would still have dead code paths (MINOR NO-NO but I can settle if necessary).</p>
<p>What are my options here? Do I truly have to resort to pre-processor abuse and wrap every affected part in the <em>alarm-module</em> with</p>
<pre><code>#ifdef USE_PROBE3
/*activity related to probe3*/
#endif
</code></pre>
<p>That seems like my only option.</p>
<p>Have I already messed up in the organization/structure of my project?
Are there any open-source projects which deal with similar issues?
Thank you in advance!</p>

Title: Bare-metal Arduino C Code won&#39;t blink LED.
Body: <p>I am trying to learn embedded C on Arduino the bare-metal, no IDE way...and I was trying out writing some of the <code>avr/io.h</code> macros myself in a different manner. I tried Blink LED by using direct memory addresses (which works) and by pointer for struct with bit-fields (<em>which doesn't work</em>).</p>
<p>The F_CPU macro is defined.
Device: Arduino UNO R3 board, which has ATMEGA328P chip.</p>
<p>Direct Memory Addresses (works as intended):</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;util/delay.h&gt;

int main (void) {

    *(volatile unsigned char*) 0x24 |= 1 &lt;&lt; 5;

    while(1) {

        *(volatile unsigned char*) 0x25 |= 1 &lt;&lt; 5;

        _delay_ms(500);

        *(volatile unsigned char*) 0x25 &amp;= ~(1 &lt;&lt; 5);

        _delay_ms(500);
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Assembly by compiler:</p>
<pre><code>    .file   &quot;main.c&quot;
__SP_H__ = 0x3e
__SP_L__ = 0x3d
__SREG__ = 0x3f
__tmp_reg__ = 0
__zero_reg__ = 1
    .text
    .section    .text.startup,&quot;ax&quot;,@progbits
.global main
    .type   main, @function
main:
/* prologue: function */
/* frame size = 0 */
/* stack size = 0 */
.L__stack_usage = 0
    sbi 0x4,5
.L2:
    sbi 0x5,5
    ldi r18,lo8(1599999)
    ldi r24,hi8(1599999)
    ldi r25,hlo8(1599999)
1:  subi r18,1
    sbci r24,0
    sbci r25,0
    brne 1b
    rjmp .
    nop
    cbi 0x5,5
    ldi r18,lo8(1599999)
    ldi r24,hi8(1599999)
    ldi r25,hlo8(1599999)
1:  subi r18,1
    sbci r24,0
    sbci r25,0
    brne 1b
    rjmp .
    nop
    rjmp .L2
    .size   main, .-main
    .ident  &quot;GCC: (Fedora 13.2.0-1.fc38) 13.2.0&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Struct with bit-fields way (doesn't work, LED is just turned ON):</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;util/delay.h&gt;

typedef unsigned char   byte;

volatile struct {
    volatile byte PINB0: 1;
    volatile byte PINB1: 1;
    volatile byte PINB2: 1;
    volatile byte PINB3: 1;
    volatile byte PINB4: 1;
    volatile byte PINB5: 1;
    volatile byte PINB6: 1;
    volatile byte PINB7: 1;
} *__ATMEGA_328P_PINB = (void*) 0x23;

volatile struct {
    volatile byte DDB0: 1;
    volatile byte DDB1: 1;
    volatile byte DDB2: 1;
    volatile byte DDB3: 1;
    volatile byte DDB4: 1;
    volatile byte DDB5: 1;
    volatile byte DDB6: 1;
    volatile byte DDB7: 1;
} *__ATMEGA_328P_DDRB = (void*) 0x24;

volatile struct {
    volatile byte PORTB0: 1;
    volatile byte PORTB1: 1;
    volatile byte PORTB2: 1;
    volatile byte PORTB3: 1;
    volatile byte PORTB4: 1;
    volatile byte PORTB5: 1;
    volatile byte PORTB6: 1;
    volatile byte PORTB7: 1;
} *__ATMEGA_328P_PORTB = (void*) 0x25;

int main (void) {

    __ATMEGA_328P_DDRB-&gt;DDB5 = 1;

    while(1) {
        __ATMEGA_328P_PORTB-&gt;PORTB5 = 1;

        _delay_ms(500);

        __ATMEGA_328P_PORTB-&gt;PORTB5 = 0;

        _delay_ms(500);
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Assembly by compiler:</p>
<pre><code>    .file   &quot;main.c&quot;
__SP_H__ = 0x3e
__SP_L__ = 0x3d
__SREG__ = 0x3f
__tmp_reg__ = 0
__zero_reg__ = 1
    .text
    .section    .text.startup,&quot;ax&quot;,@progbits
.global main
    .type   main, @function
main:
/* prologue: function */
/* frame size = 0 */
/* stack size = 0 */
.L__stack_usage = 0
    lds r30,__ATMEGA_328P_DDRB
    lds r31,__ATMEGA_328P_DDRB+1
    ld r24,Z
