Title: How to automate Java code refactoring using a script with instructions?.
Body: <p>How to refactor this class programmatically via Java code/Groovy plugin?
Let's say, I need to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Rename <code>foo.method2</code> to <code>foo.method3</code></li>
<li>Rename <code>myMethod</code> to <code>yourMethod</code></li>
<li>Change the imported package <code>org.you.core.util.AnotherClassFromExternalPackage</code></li>
</ul>
<pre><code>import com.me.core.util.AnotherClassFromExternalPackage;
import com.me.core.util.Foo;

public class MyClass implements AnotherClassFromExternalPackage {

    private final Foo foo;

    public MyClass() {
        this.foo = new Foo();
    }

    @Override
    public long myMethod() {
        return foo.method2();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>How to create a script that will parse the code, apply syntactical transformations and save it using given instructions?</p>
<p>The code above is just an example. The bigger problem is that I have a lot of projects that are using the same external library. And sometimes they release a new version with breaking changes that is breaking the current code after the dependency bump.
And I must update the dependency to the latest version every 2 weeks for 10+ projects. I need to write a script that will fix these breaking changes automatically.
Ideally, apply code transformations one by one.</p>

Title: Using Context inside a Class Instance in React.js.
Body: <p>I'm trying to use <a href="https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns/template-method" rel="nofollow noreferrer">template design pattern</a>, to refactor a complex business logic in my React application. I have 3-4 flows where-in some steps are common, but have slightly different implementations or addition and intermediate steps.</p>
<p>For this I'm creating an abstract base class in TypeScript with concrete implementation of few steps. The Classes that will extend this abstract class needs to provide concrete implementation of the abstract methods defined inside base class.</p>
<p>Now, the problem/query I'm having is regarding using Context API and Hooks(like useHistory) inside these Plain TypeScript classes (that are not extending <code>React.Component</code>).</p>
<p>The code looks something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { MyContext } from './common/utils/MyContext';
import { useHistory } from 'react-router-dom';
import * as React from 'react';

const MyComponent = () =&gt; {
  const history = useHistory();
  const context = React.useContext(MyContext);

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (foo &amp;&amp; bar) {
      const flowInitializer1 = new flowInitializer1(history, context, foo, bar);
      flowInitializer1.initiate();
    } else if (foo &amp;&amp; bazz) {
      const flowInitializer2 = new flowInitializer2(history, context, foo, bazz);
      flowInitializer2.initiate();
    } else {
      const flowInitializer3 = new flowInitializer3(history, context);
      flowInitializer3.initiate();
      flowInitializer3.cleanup();
    }
  }, []);

  return (
    // some JSX
  );
}

abstract class BaseFlowInitializer {
    constructor(
      context: IContextType,
      history:  RouteComponentProps['history']
    ) {}

    step1() {
      this.context.foo();
      // some code 
    }

    step2() {
      // some code
      history.push('/fizzbuz');
    }

    abstract step3();

    abstract initiate();
}

export class flowInitializer1 extends BaseFlowInitializer {
  step3() {
    // concrete implementation
  }

  initiate() {
    // concrete implementation
  }
}

export class flowInitializer2 extends BaseFlowInitializer {
  step3() {
    // concrete implementation
  }

  step4() {

  }

  initiate() {
    // concrete implementation
  }
}

export class flowInitializer3 extends BaseFlowInitializer {
  step3() {
    // concrete implementation
  }

  cleanup() {
    // some additional step
  }

  initiate() {
    // concrete implementation
  }
}

</code></pre>
<p>My Questions now are:</p>
<ol>
<li>Is the above implementation recommended as per React standards?</li>
<li>Can the above code cause performance issues, by breaking the React Fibre?</li>
<li>Is there any better way to implement template design pattern in React.js?</li>
</ol>

Title: How to make this awk script less boilerplate?.
Body: <p>Suppose I've got a log file like this (1st column is a timestamp):</p>
<pre><code>1699740442177 Start A
1699740442177 Start B
1699740442255 Start C
1699740442261 Finish B
1699740442337 Finish C
1699740442337 Finish A
</code></pre>
<p>I want to determine how much time in average it took for <code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, and <code>C</code>.  So I wrote an <code>awk</code> script :</p>
<pre><code>/Start A/ {
  startA = $1
}
/Finish A/ {
  sumA += $1 - startA
  numA++ 
}
/Start B/ {
  startB = $1
}
/Finish B/ {
  sumB += $1 - startB
  numB++ 
}
/Start C/ {
  startC = $1
}
/Finish C/ {
  sumC += $1 - startC
  numC++ 
}
END {
  print sumA/numA, sumB/numB, sumC/numC
}
</code></pre>
<p>The script works but it is somewhat <em>boilerplate</em>. How would you suggest improve the script and make it less boilerplate ?</p>
<p>The number of letters may vary (10-15 letters max). We can assume there is no overlap between <code>Start/Finish</code> pairs for the same letter.</p>

Title: java refactoring - moving enum to a different package without changing imports.
Body: <p><em>Update: <strong>I'm really not looking for tips how to change company processes</strong>, how to throw the problem at others, etc. Rather, <strong>I was hoping to find if there is some obscure bit of Java knowledge</strong> that would allow me to abuse Enums for a temporary workaround. After asking this question, <strong>I managed to</strong> find a quieter period and <strong>release my change</strong>. But I'm still curious if there is something in the dark corners of Java that could be helpful in a situation like this.</em></p>
<p>I have a huge, old (but still actively developed) codebase in Java with dozens of packages and I need to move an enum between them.</p>
<p>There is this enum that's inside of a class:</p>
<pre><code>package com.foo.bar;
public class ParentClass
{
    public Enum MyEnum
    {
        ONE, TWO;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This enum is widely used across the entire application. I need to extract it from the parent class and move it into a different package:</p>
<pre><code>package com.enums;
public Enum MyEnum
{
    ONE, TWO;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The standard way how to do it is a simple refactoring with the use of IDE. Which works. But... This leads to changes in many places and because the codebase is actively developed by different teams and many people, I keep getting merge conflicts, new usages of this enum are appearing, and so on. So I'm stuck in a whack-a-mole situation. I bring my change up to date, run test suites and build it, but before that is done (the whole loop takes at least a day, not speaking about a new code review), I end up with another conflict. Rinse, repeat.</p>
<p>Telling everyone to stop working while I merge this is not possible. If it was a normal class and not an enum, I could try to use inheritance: move it to the new location and replace the original location with an empty child class, and then deal with the usages one by one. But Java enums can't be extended.</p>
<p>Is there any way how I could break this change into multiple stages (so I do not need to change all the imports where it is used) instead of having to do it all at once, in an atomic change?</p>

Title: Refactor an Object that expects one thing to expect many things.
Body: <p>I am working on updating a Go codebase. Our company has a central data store, fictionally called DataStore, that you can publish to or read from various datasets or schemas. Our particular code base has some code that couples a specific dataset/schema to a DataStore implementation. I need to refactor this code to enable writing to an additional (and potentially many more) datasets.</p>
<p>In the code below, <code>dataStoreSchema</code> represents a single schema, like &quot;Customers&quot; data. But I have other schemas like &quot;Orders&quot;, or whatever that have different fields that I would like to write to.</p>
<p><code>transformEvent</code> takes data that would be passed to <code>DataStore</code> and does some logic to create the <code>dataStoreSchema</code> object which then gets written to the actual DataStore in the cloud.</p>
<p>I want to write to potentially <em>any</em> schema on the data store, not just the one that this implementation is coupled to.</p>
<pre><code>type Parameters struct {
    SchemaName              string
    Host                    string
    Secret                  string
}

type DataStore struct {
    params                *Parameters
    url                   string
    request               *http.Client
}

// imagine this defines an Order, but other schemas might be for Customer
type dataStoreSchema struct {
    eventUrl string `json:&quot;url&quot;`
    objectID string `json:&quot;object_id&quot;`
}

func New(parameters) (*DataStore, error) {
    // implementation

    return &amp;stream, nil
}

// takes input and converts it into a schema object that can be written to the datastore
func (ds *DataStore) transformEvent(...) dataStoreSchema {

    // .... implementation

    return dataStoreSchema{
        eventUrl: url,
        objectID: objectId,
    }
}

func (ds *DataStore) writeEvents(...) error {

    // .... implementation
    payload := ds.transformEvent(event)


    ds.produce(payload)
}

func (ds *DataStore) produce(msg []events) {
        ds.WriteToDatastore(msg)
}

</code></pre>
<p>Right now the behavior is this</p>
<pre><code>myDatasetClient := DataStore.New(params)
myDatasetClient.write(&lt;-messages chan)
</code></pre>
<p>But I'd like to be able to do something like this</p>
<pre><code>myDatasetClient1 := DataStore.New(params{schema1, transformEvent1})
myDatasetClient2 := DataStore.New(params{schema2, transformEvent2})
myDatasetClient3 := DataStore.New(params{schema3, transformEvent3})
</code></pre>
<p>Or whatever makes the most sense in Go terms</p>

