Title: Is lack of vectorized instructions for std::ranges::transform an issue with my expectations, the library, the optimizer, or the standard?.
Body: <p>Consider the following code:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &lt;ranges&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

std::vector&lt;int&gt; fn1(std::vector&lt;int&gt; u, std::vector&lt;int&gt; const&amp; v) {
    std::transform(u.begin(), u.end(), v.begin(), u.begin(), std::plus&lt;int&gt;{});
    return u;
}
std::vector&lt;int&gt; fn2(std::vector&lt;int&gt; u, std::vector&lt;int&gt; const&amp; v) {
    std::ranges::transform(u, v, u.begin(), std::plus&lt;int&gt;{});
    return u;
} 
</code></pre>
<p>In gcc 12 -O3, <code>fn1</code> produces code that is vectorized, whereas <code>fn2</code> does not.  This difference can cause non-trivial performance implications.  I consider this a serious issue.  However, given the specification for <code>std::ranges::transform</code>, I cannot point to a specific defect.  In my opinion, it is borderline outrageous for &quot;modern&quot; C++ to have this performance caveat for <code>std::ranges::transform</code>.</p>

Title: How to decompress bit pairs from uint64_t to __m256i?.
Body: <p>Consider <code>uint64_t</code> where each consecutive 2 bits is a number: <code>b00</code> for <code>0</code>, <code>b01</code> for <code>1</code>, <code>b11</code> for <code>-1</code> and <code>b10</code> is unused (never happens, assume no handling for it).
How to decompress such an <code>uint64_t</code> into <code>__m256i</code> so that bit pairs from the original number become signed bytes in the vector variable? I need to unpack <code>b00</code> into <code>b00000000</code>, <code>b01</code> into <code>b00000001</code> and <code>b11</code> into <code>b11111111</code>, while preserving the order.</p>
<p>What I have tried so far:
Inspired by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/76946161/1915854">this answer</a>, I only have come up with the inverse procedure, where bytes of <code>__m256i</code> are packed into bit pairs of an <code>uint64_t</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const __m256i vBits0 = _mm256_slli_epi16(target, 7);
const int bits1 = _mm256_movemask_epi8(target);
const int bits0 = _mm256_movemask_epi8(vBits0);
const uint64_t withHigher = _pdep_u64(bits1, 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA);
const uint64_t withLower =  _pdep_u64(bits0, 0x5555555555555555);
return withHigher | withLower;
</code></pre>
<p>I have access to AVX2, but AVX512 would be also of interest.</p>

Title: How do you Associate/Dissociate an HTS token using EVM transaction?.
Body: <p>Before I can receive an HTS token I need to first associate with the token ID.
If I were using the JS SDK I know to do the following where the <code>accountId</code> is the account that I want to associate with the token.</p>
<pre><code>  const associateTransaction = await new TokenAssociateTransaction()
    .setAccountId(accountId)
    .setTokenIds([tokenId])
