Title: Dynamic programming technique.
Body: <p>I have this problem that I'm trying to solve:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Given two numbers <code>K</code> and <code>S</code>, determine how many different values of <code>X</code>, <code>Y</code>, and <code>Z</code> exist such that <code>0 ≤ X, Y, Z ≤ K</code> and <code>X + Y + Z = S</code>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Now, what I did is a recursive solution that tries every possible value for each one of the three digits.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int count_sum(int k,int s,int sum=0,int limit=1,int counter=0)
{
    if(sum&gt;s)
        return 0;

    if(sum==s)
        return 1;

    if(limit&gt;3)
        return 0;

    for(int i=0; i&lt;=k; i++)
    {
        if(i&gt;s)
            break;
        counter=counter + count_sum(k,s,sum+i,limit+1);
    }
    return counter;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I know that this is <code>O(k^3)</code>, which is too high. I also know that there is a hack to solve this problem in <code>O(k^2)</code>. However, what I'm trying to do is optimize this solution with the technique of dynamic programming. I have tried to sketch a tree, but I couldn't figure out how to optimize it.</p>

Title: Finding the sum of Digits expressed as a string of Letters.
Body: <p>I have a string <code>str</code> of lowercase letters in the range <code>[a-z]</code>.
Few letters of the string when combined together, represent a digit from <code>[0-9]</code> i.e. &quot;zero&quot; = 0, &quot;one&quot; = 1, &quot;two&quot; = 2 and so on.</p>
<p>The characters in the string are jumbled. However, it is guaranteed that all the characters in the string can be used to form a set of digits. Some digits can be repeated as well. There are no invalid characters such as <code>a</code>, <code>p</code>, <code>q</code> etc.</p>
<p>My task is to find the sum of the digits represented by <code>str</code> .</p>
<p>Examples :<br />
<code>str</code> = &quot;oneeno&quot; should output <code>2 (1+1)</code><br />
<code>str</code> = &quot;onewtofoursevne&quot; should output <code>14 (1+2+4+7)</code></p>
<p>let <code>solve(s)</code> represent solving for a particular sub-string of <code>str</code> .<br />
i have an array of strings, <code>v</code> to store the spellings of the digits. v = {&quot;zero&quot;, &quot;one&quot;, &quot;two&quot;, ... &quot;nine&quot;};</p>
<p>I will break down <code>s</code> and add the digit value, wherever I am able to find a set of characters representing a digit.</p>
<pre><code>if (contains(s, v[i]) == true)
    dp[s-v[i]] = i + solve(s-v[i])
</code></pre>
<p><code>s-v[i]</code> represents the set of characters in <code>v[i]</code> removed from <code>s</code> . As it is not possible to subtract strings in that manner, I substitute <code>s-v[i]</code> with <code>change(s, v[i])</code> (in my code).</p>
<p>This seems like a recursive function with multiple repeating sub-states.<br />
For example : <code>solve(s - v[1] - v[4] - v[7]) = solve(s - v[4] - v[1] - v[7])</code> .</p>
<p>I've used Top-Down Dynamic Programming for this where <code>dp[s-v[i]]</code> is the state of <code>s</code> when characters representing <code>v[i]</code>  are removed from it. Instead of removing characters, I simply replace the used characters with a <code>$</code> symbol. If the entire string has the <code>$</code> character, it means we've found a valid solution (which forms the base case).</p>
<p>As the strings are jumbled, I use a map of strings to store the sub-states and the strings are always sorted to prevent any redundancy.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;
using ll =  long long;

vector&lt;string&gt; v = {&quot;zero&quot;, &quot;one&quot;, &quot;two&quot;, &quot;three&quot;, &quot;four&quot;, &quot;five&quot;, &quot;six&quot;, &quot;seven&quot;, &quot;eight&quot;, &quot;nine&quot;};
map&lt;string, int&gt; dp;
bool b;

bool check(string str, int n) {
    string t = v[n];
    sort(t.begin(), t.end()); sort(str.begin(), str.end());

    int i=0, j=0, k=0;
    while(i &lt; t.size() &amp;&amp; j &lt; str.size()) {
        if(t[i] == str[j]) {
            ++i; ++j; ++k;
        }
        else ++j;
    }

    return (k == t.size()) ? true : false;   
}

string change(string str, int n) {
    string t = v[n];
    sort(t.begin(), t.end());
    sort(str.begin(), str.end());

    int i=0, j=0;
    while(i &lt; t.size() &amp;&amp; j &lt; str.size()) {
        if(t[i] == str[j]) {
            str[j] = '$';
            ++i; ++j;
        }
        else ++j;
    }
    sort(str.begin(), str.end());
    return str;
}

int solve(string str, map&lt;string, int&gt;&amp; dp) {

    if(str[str.size()-1] == '$') {
        b = true; // a solution exists
        return 0;
    }

    if(dp.find(str) != dp.end()) // if state is already computed
        return dp[str];

    for(int i=0; i&lt;10; i++) {
        if(check(str, i)) {
            return dp[change(str, i)] = i + solve(change(str, i), dp);
        }
    }

    return dp[str] = 0;
}

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(0);
    cin.tie(0); cout.tie(0);

    #ifndef ONLINE_JUDGE
        freopen(&quot;input&quot;, &quot;r&quot;, stdin);
        freopen(&quot;output&quot;, &quot;w&quot;, stdout);
    #endif

    string str; cin &gt;&gt; str;
    sort(str.begin(), str.end());

    b = false; 

    int res = 0;

    for(int i=0; i&lt;10; i++) {
        if(check(str, i)) {
            dp[change(str, i)] = i + solve(change(str, i), dp);
        }
        if(b) { // valid solution found
            res = dp[change(str, i)];
            break; 
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; res;    
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The code works alright for smaller strings :<br />
Example : <code>str = &quot;towsnveefourfive&quot;</code> yields <code>18 (2 + 7 + 4 + 5)</code><br />
However, for larger strings such as <code>str = &quot;towsnveefourfiveone&quot;</code> yields <code>0</code> instead of <code>19</code></p>
<p>What could be the error in my approach ?</p>

Title: Finding Maximal-Sum Positive Integer Subarray.
Body: <p>You are given an integer array consisting of N positive integers. You have to pick a subarray of length K. Subarray means a contiguous part of an array. You need to find the sum of the subarray with the maximal sum among all such subarrays.</p>
<p>Input should be given as follows. On the first line, N and K: the size of the array, the size of the subarray to choose. On the next line: N integers, Ai.
The output should be the answer, the sum of the subarray that has the maximal sum.</p>
<p>Constraints
<code>1 ≤ K ≤ N ≤ 1000</code> and <code>1 ≤ Ai ≤ 1000</code></p>
<p>I solved it like this but 2 tests give errors. What have I overlooked?</p>
<pre><code>#include&lt;iostream&gt;
using namespace std;
int main(){
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(NULL);
    cin.tie(NULL);
    int n,k;
    cin&gt;&gt;n&gt;&gt;k;
    int a,s[n],sum=0,ans=0,ans1;
    for(int i=0; i&lt;n; i++){
        cin&gt;&gt;a;
        sum+=a; 
        s[i]=sum;
    }
    if(k==n){
      cout&lt;&lt;sum&lt;&lt;endl;
      return 0;
    }
    for(int i=n-1; i&gt;=0; i--){
       if(i-k&gt;=0){
         ans1=s[i]-s[i-k];
       }
         ans=max(ans,ans1);
    }
    cout&lt;&lt;ans&lt;&lt;endl;
    return 0;}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://algoleague.com/problem/max-subarray-2/detail" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://algoleague.com/problem/max-subarray-2/detail</a></p>

Title: Memoization with recursion slower than just recursion.
Body: <p>I'm trying to understand time complexity-wise what to expect running fibonacci with recursion vs. recursion with memoization &amp; recursion.</p>
<p>I expect using only recursion - (2^n) - to be slower than with memoization <em><strong>and</strong></em> recursion - O(n). That is, memoization is one way to do dynamic programming, an optimization technique...so, I'd expect it to be faster.</p>
<p>First, run using recursion.</p>
<p>Time: <strong>10</strong>ms   leetcode:  beats 23%</p>
<pre><code>public int fib(int n) {
    if (n &lt; 2) {
        return n;
    }
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)
}
</code></pre>
<p>Second, run with memoization <em><strong>and</strong></em> recursion.</p>
<p>Time: <strong>29</strong>ms leetcode: beats 13%</p>
<pre><code>public int fib(int n) {
    if (n &lt; 2) {
        return n;
    } 
    int[] dp = new int[n+1];
    if (dp[n] != 0){
        return dp[n];  
    } 
    int result = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
    dp[n] = result;
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can see that using recursion alone is faster. Why?</p>

