Title: 3D Heat Equation in MATLAB.
Body: <p>I am trying to model in MATLAB the temperature distribution inside a rectangular prism with <a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/9AJmd.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">boundary and initial conditions and heat equation</a> I was trying to visualize 2D slices in the 3D shape. However, the simulation takes too long and when I try to change the time step, I get stability issues and the output visuals look weird and inconsistent. Is there a way to fix this? The code I used is below:</p>
<pre><code>clear; clf;

rho = 1;
cp = 1;
k = 1;

alpha = rho / (cp * k);

Lx = 10;
Ly = 10;
Lz = 10;

Nx = 21; Nt = 35*60;
Ny = 21;
Nz = 21;
dx = Lx / (Nx - 1);
dy = Ly / (Ny - 1);
dz = Lz / (Nz - 1);

c = 1;
C = 0.1;
dt = C * dx / c;

Tn = zeros(Nx, Ny, Nz);
x = linspace(0, Lx, Nx);
y = linspace(0, Ly, Ny);
z = linspace(0, Lz, Nz);
[X, Y, Z] = meshgrid(x, y, z);

Tn(:, :, :) = 80;
t = 0;

Tn(1, :, :) = 350; Tn(end, :, :) = 350;
Tn(:, 1, :) = 350; Tn(:, end, :) = 350;
Tn(:, :, 1) = 350; Tn(:, :, end) = 350;

figure;
title(sprintf('Time = %f seconds', t));

for n = 1:Nt
    Tc = Tn;

    t = t + dt;
    for i = 2:Nx - 1
        for j = 2:Ny - 1
            for k = 2:Nz - 1
                Tn(i,j,k)=Tc(i,j,k) +...
                    dt * alpha *...
                    (((Tc(i+1,j,k) - 2*Tc(i,j,k) + Tc(i-1,j,k))/dx/dx)+...
                    ((Tc(i,j+1,k) - 2*Tc(i,j,k) + Tc(i,j-1,k))/dy/dy)+...
                    ((Tc(i,j,k+1) - 2*Tc(i,j,k) + Tc(i,j,k-1))/dz/dz));
            end
        end
    end

    % Display slices in the XZ plane
    slice(X, Y, Z, Tn, [], Lx/2, []);

    xlabel('X (inches)');
    ylabel('Y (inches)');
    zlabel('Z (inches)');
    title(sprintf('Time = %f seconds', t));

    % Pause for a short duration to visualize the changes
    pause(0.1);
end

</code></pre>
<p>I was trying to get an image like <a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/zXsiF.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this</a>. However, the simulation takes too long and after a short time there does not seem to be any temperature change whatsoever. When I increase the time step, I get a <a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/g7XS9.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">weird result</a>. Do I have to wait for the simulation to take its time? And are there inconsistencies or flaws in my implementation of the heat equation in MATLAB?</p>

Title: Simulation mismatch when using shortreal + shortrealtobits + bitstoshortreal combination in modelsim tool.
Body: <p>Here is the minimal reproducible problem:</p>
<p>file: top.sv</p>
<pre><code>module top(input [31:0] in1, output [31:0] out1);
    assign out1 = in1;
endmodule
</code></pre>
<p>file top_tb.sv</p>
<pre><code>module top_tb;

shortreal in1_real;
shortreal out1_tb;
shortreal out_dut;

logic [31:0] in1_logic;
logic [31:0] out1_logic;
logic check;

initial in1_real = 2.1;
initial out1_tb = 2.1;


assign #5 in1_logic = $shortrealtobits(in1_real);

top inst_top(.in1(in1_logic),
          .out1(out1_logic));

assign #10 out_dut = $bitstoshortreal(out1_logic);
assign #30 check = 1;

always_comb begin
    if ((check == 1) &amp;&amp; (out1_tb != out_dut)) begin
        $display(&quot;%t Not matching!!&quot;, $time);
    end
end

endmodule
</code></pre>
<p>When I run a simulation of this dut and tb in modelsim, I am not expecting any mismatch between <code>out1_tb</code> and <code>out_dut</code>, but I am seeing a mismatch. When I checked the waveform, both <code>out1_tb</code> and <code>out_dut</code> are shown as 2.1 only, still I am seeing a simulation mismatch.</p>
<p>Link to EDA Playground with the design and tesbench code: <a href="https://edaplayground.com/x/WDiC" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://edaplayground.com/x/WDiC</a></p>
<p>When you click &quot;run&quot;, you can see &quot;Not matching&quot; in the log.</p>

Title: I ran the exact same code twice and got different outputs.
Body: <p>I'm working on a simulation in R to model some human behavior and for some reason, my simulation is alternating between two different result patterns. Below is the code for the two simulations that I'm running. 10000 iterations should be sufficient to converge on a single pattern, and yet, that is not what is happening. I'm banging my head against a wall trying to figure out what is happening--I would love any and all assistance!</p>
<p>EDIT:
It was suggested that I add some additional details about what I'm trying to simulate, here are those details!</p>
<p>I'm modeling a so-called &quot;balloon-popping paradigm&quot; where one is tasked with estimating a value (variable name i2 and is3 here) and getting as close as possible to a true value (randNum2 and randNum3) without going over or under that value. I'm trying to model how this process works when there is variability in the true value. This variability is coming from multiple sources which is why I have multiple runif functions being added together.</p>
<p>The expected converging pattern is that when there is more variability in the true value, the simulation should result in a more conservative estimate. Sometimes when I run this, I get that pattern, othertimes, I get a pattern where regardless of the amount of variability, the simulation converges on the same value.</p>
<pre><code>
OsimulationShort &lt;- data.frame(timeEstimate = double(), averagePayout = double())
for (i2 in seq(from = 1, to = 25, by = .5)){
  temp2 &lt;- data.frame(timeEstimate = double(), payout = double())
  for (n2 in 1:10000){
      randNum2 &lt;- runif(n=1,min=3,max=7) + runif(n=1,min=-1,max=1)#+ rnorm(n=1,m = 0, sd = 1) 
      if (i2 &gt; randNum2){
         reward2 = 0
      } else{
         reward2 = i2
      }
  temp2 &lt;- rbind(temp2,data.frame(timeEstimate = i2, payout = reward2))    
  }  
  OsimulationShort &lt;- rbind(OsimulationShort,data.frame(timeEstimate = i2, averagePayout = mean(temp2$payout)))
}

optimal &lt;- rbind(optimal, data.frame(timeLongOrShort = &quot;Short&quot;, distType = &quot;Variability&quot;, riskCondition = &quot;NO OVER&quot;, opTime = OsimulationShort$timeEstimate[OsimulationShort$averagePayout == max(OsimulationShort$averagePayout)]))


UsimulationLong &lt;- data.frame(timeEstimate = double(), averagePayout = double())
for (i3 in seq(from = 1, to = 25, by = .5)){
  temp3 &lt;- data.frame(timeEstimate = double(), payout = double())
  for (n3 in 1:10000){
      randNum3 &lt;- runif(n=1,min=9,max=21) + runif(n=1,min=-3,max=3)#+ rnorm(n=1,m = 0, sd =3) 
      if (i3 &lt; randNum3){
         reward3 = 0
      } else{
         reward3 = (2*randNum3) -i3
      }
  temp3 &lt;- rbind(temp3,data.frame(timeEstimate = i3, payout = reward3))    
  }  
  UsimulationLong &lt;- rbind(UsimulationLong,data.frame(timeEstimate = i3, averagePayout = mean(temp3$payout)))
}

optimal &lt;- rbind(optimal, data.frame(timeLongOrShort = &quot;Long&quot;, distType = &quot;Variability&quot;, riskCondition = &quot;NO UNDER&quot;, opTime = UsimulationLong$timeEstimate[UsimulationLong$averagePayout == max(UsimulationLong$averagePayout)]))

</code></pre>
<p>I ran the exact same code twice in a row, and got vastly different results.</p>

Title: How to export to dict some properties of an object.
Body: <p>I have a python class which has several properties. I want to implement a method which will return some properties as a dict. I want to mark the properties with a decorator. Here's an example:</p>
<pre><code>class Foo:
    @export_to_dict  # I want to add this property to dict
    @property
    def bar1(self):
        return 1

    @property # I don't want to add this propetry to dict
    def bar2(self):
        return {&quot;smth&quot;: 2}

    @export_to_dict # I want to add this property to dict
    @property
    def bar3(self):
        return &quot;a&quot;

    @property
    def bar4(self):
        return [2, 3, 4]

    def to_dict(self):
        return ... # expected result: {&quot;bar1&quot;: 1, &quot;bar3&quot;: &quot;a&quot;}
</code></pre>
<p>One way to implement it is to set an additional attribute to the properties with the export_to_dict decorator, like this:</p>
<pre><code>def export_to_dict(func):
    setattr(func, '_export_to_dict', True)
    return func
</code></pre>
<p>and to search for properties with the <code>_export_to_dict</code> attribute when <code>to_dict</code> is called.</p>
<p>Is there another way to accomplish the task?</p>

Title: ClassDecorator with Angular (15) targeting ES2022.
Body: <p>We currently are in march 2023 and I am looking for a way to make my class decorators work the right way, with no warning from the cli.</p>
<p>Here my simple code :</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function MyDecorator(myConstructor: new (...args: any[]) =&gt; any): new (...args: any[]) =&gt; any {
  alert('MyDecorator EVALUATE'); // -------------&gt; Alert A
  return class extends myConstructor {
    constructor(...args) {
      super(...args);
      alert('MyDecorator CONSTRUCTOR'); // ------------&gt; Alert B
    }
  };
}

@MyDecorator
@Component({
  // ...
})
export class AppComponent() {
  // ...
} 
</code></pre>
<h2>Before Angular 15:</h2>
<p>I was targeting ES2021 or lower and this worked perfectly. I got alerts A &amp; B and the cli gave no warning.</p>
<h2>With Angular 15:</h2>
<p>I was still targeting ES2021 and it was still working. I got alerts A &amp; B</p>
<p><strong>BUT</strong> the cli warned me it overrided some settings.</p>
<pre><code>TypeScript compiler options &quot;target&quot; and &quot;useDefineForClassFields&quot; are set to &quot;ES2022&quot; and &quot;false&quot; respectively by the Angular CLI. To control ECMA version and features use the Browerslist configuration. For more information, see https://angular.io/guide/build#configuring-browser-compatibility
</code></pre>
<p>I tried to set these two settings in the <code>tsconfig.json</code></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>    &quot;target&quot;: &quot;ES2022&quot;,
    &quot;useDefineForClassFields&quot;: false,
</code></pre>
<p>Then I had no more cli warning <strong>BUT</strong> I only got the alert A and my decorator implementation is clearly ignored (no alert B was displayed).</p>
<p>It looks like that the <code>&quot;useDefineForClassFields&quot;: false</code> is ignored (cf : <a href="https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/48276#issuecomment-1362787912" rel="noreferrer">https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/48276#issuecomment-1362787912</a>)</p>
<p>Well my 2 questions are :</p>
<ul>
<li>is there a way to make decorators work &quot;natively&quot; in Angular 15/ES2022 ?</li>
<li>if not, is there a way to properly set the <code>useDefineForClassFields</code> compilerOption to avoid the warning of the cli ?</li>
</ul>
<p>Thanks in advance for any help/explanation ...</p>

Title: How to Prevent Nested Decorators in Autofac When Using Interface Inheritance?.
Body: <p>I have a .NET 7.0 application where I am using Autofac as my DI container. I have two interfaces <code>IReadonlyRepository&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>IRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>, where <code>IRepository&lt;T&gt;</code> inherits from <code>IReadonlyRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>My Repository classes implement these interfaces. I also have a decorator, <code>LoggingRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>, which wraps <code>IRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>, logs each call to the repository's methods, and delegates the call to the wrapped <code>IRepository&lt;T&gt;</code> instance. Here is the code for <code>LoggingRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>class LoggingRepository&lt;T&gt; : IRepository&lt;T&gt;
{
    private readonly IRepository&lt;T&gt; _repository;

    public LoggingRepository(IRepository&lt;T&gt; repository)
    {
        _repository = repository;
    }

    public IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; List()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($&quot;Calling {_repository.GetType().Name}&lt;{typeof(T).Name}&gt;.List()&quot;);
        return _repository.List();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here's how I register my classes and decorators in the DI container:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>builder.RegisterGeneric(typeof(Repository&lt;&gt;))
    .As(typeof(IRepository&lt;&gt;))
    .As(typeof(IReadonlyRepository&lt;&gt;))
    .InstancePerLifetimeScope();

builder.RegisterGenericDecorator(typeof(LoggingRepository&lt;&gt;), typeof(IRepository&lt;&gt;));
builder.RegisterGenericDecorator(typeof(LoggingRepository&lt;&gt;), typeof(IReadonlyRepository&lt;&gt;));
</code></pre>
<p>I have also tried using named registration to potentially circumvent this issue, but it didn't change the behavior.</p>
<p>The problem I am facing is that when I request an instance of <code>IReadonlyRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>, it appears that <code>LoggingRepository&lt;T&gt;</code> ends up decorating another instance of <code>LoggingRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>, which in turn decorates the <code>Repository&lt;T&gt;</code> instance. This is not the intended behavior - I only want a single instance of <code>LoggingRepository&lt;T&gt;</code> to decorate <code>Repository&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>In effect, the system seems to be nesting decorators because IRepository also implements IReadonlyRepository.</p>
<p>How can I prevent Autofac from nesting decorators in this situation? What's the proper way to register these decorators when dealing with interface inheritance to ensure they are not nested?</p>

